set. So where does the uninit opacity come from?\n\nLook at the MSan origin: OpenCache -> MagickRealloc. The cache for the image. The image's pixels get written in ReadTIFFImage. If all pixels are written including opacity, no uninit. Unless... the image has MORE columns/rows than written. Consider: the input is a multi-frame TIFF or the image->columns is larger than what's actually written.\n\nActually wait — reconsider. The `q` returned by SetImagePixels might not cover the whole row if the image cache isn't in sync. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider the origin more carefully: \"Uninitialized value was created by a heap allocation ... OpenCache ... ModifyCache ... SetCacheNexus ... SetCacheViewPixels ... ReadTIFFImage tiff.c:2994\". So at line 2994, SetImagePixels -> ModifyCache -> OpenCache -> MagickRealloc. The cache is allocated. The uninitialized bytes are within this cache. Then later the pixels are written. If a pixel's opacity is not written, it stays uninitialized.\n\nSo indeed, some pixels' opacity are left unwritten. How? Because the loop writes `q->opacity` only `if (image->matte)`. But matte is True... unless at the point of writing, matte was True, but the ResizeImage path... no.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is different: the input has multiple TIFF directories (frames). The first frame is read and its cache allocated. Then a subsequent frame overwrites image->columns/rows etc. and reuses the cache? If a smaller frame is read into a cache sized for a bigger frame, some pixels remain unwritten. Then ResizeImage reads them. Hmm, but SetImagePixels reallocates.\n\nActually — let me look at the real fix. The description: \"the opacity channel is not assured to be initialized in the RGBAStrippedMethod case.\" The fix probably adds `else q->opacity=OpaqueOpacity;` — i.e., the bug is when `image->matte` is False! If matte is False, opacity is never set, leaving it uninitialized. But then how does ResizeImage's VerticalFilter read it? Because `source->matte` might become